## Chapter Two: Available Response

*Available response is not contact. The distinction is the distance across which care can still be reached.*

— Adaptive Care Architecture Standard 7 (revised)

---

The anxiety woke before B did.

At 5:43, his hand crossed the dark, found the capsule beside the bed, and carried it to his mouth with the competence of a much better man.

He swallowed using water that had spent the night becoming room temperature.

"Dose recorded," Mote said quietly.

"Don't narrate."

"You are not fully conscious."

"Then who are you talking to?"

"The part of you administering an unregulated stimulant."

"That part doesn't like you."

"This has been accounted for."

B pulled the blanket over his face.

The room accepted this as a temporary objection.

He trusted stupid chemistry.

Modern stimulants wanted context. They knew who prescribed them, who paid for them, how many billable hours remained in the quarter, and whether elevated attention was being spent on approved work. They adjusted release around labor agreements. They softened during mandated rest. Some produced melancholy when used outside a recognized productivity profile.

They did not make a person energetic.

They made the energy legible.

B's capsules knew nothing. They carried no telemetry, adaptive release logic, or concern for the future.

Perfectly stupid chemistry.

He closed his eyes.

Sleep returned quickly because sleep had not yet understood it was being evicted.

The capsule opened.

His pulse climbed beneath the blanket. Blood moved faster through limbs that remained heavy and misplaced. His jaw tightened. His breathing shortened by fractions.

Somewhere below language, an animal received notice that catastrophe had been scheduled for morning.

B slept through the announcement.

In the dream, he was already dressed.

Coat on. Shoes tied. Credentials warm against his wrist.

He stood in a corridor furnished like a room. A kettle screamed on a table without ever boiling. Unanswered correspondence floated face-down in a sink built into one wall. A chair turned slowly toward him, carrying the impression that someone had just stood up and left because he had taken too long.

At the far end, an open doorway revealed only brightness.

B walked toward it.

The floor moved beneath him in the opposite direction.

Somewhere beyond the doorway, a departure bell rang.

The corridor stretched.

Then, without crossing the distance, he was standing in a train station.

Platforms extended beneath a roof too high to belong indoors. Trains waited with their doors open. None displayed a destination.

An attendant looked down at his wrist.

"Sir, you are late."

"For what?"

She pointed above him.

The timetable clicked through rows of blank departures and settled on a single word.

EVERYTHING.

B woke.

"B," Mote said.

He turned away from the voice.

"Your pulse is elevated."

"You poisoned me."

"You administered the capsule."

"Then arrest me later."

"A residential support field has entered a positive feedback condition."

The sentence reached him where gentler language would have failed.

He opened one eye.

"What kind?"

"Morrow Stack. Assisted living, east residential tier. Its adaptive-care systems have generalized support into continuous physical contact."

B opened the other eye.

"How continuous?"

"One resident is pinned against a bedroom ceiling. Two cannot separate from their furniture. A fourth is being followed by a handrail."

"Followed?"

"The handrail has extended through three rooms to remain within reach."

B sat up too quickly.

The room arrived in sections. Bed. Window. Floor. His own knees, farther away than expected.

"Who owns the maintenance contract?"

"Three entities are disputing responsibility."

"Which means all three billed them last month."

"Yes."

"The emergency rate?"

"Twelve times your standard."

"Accept."

"Accepted."

The diagnostic glass beside the bed filled with the architecture of another person's pain.

B felt the morning become useful.

He put both feet on the floor before he could examine that too closely.

His body considered this an escalation.

The stimulant had made him urgent without making him coordinated. His heart was awake. His hands were still asleep. The distinction seemed mostly administrative.

He crossed to the workroom in his underwear.

Morning had not yet reached Meridian.

Beyond the glass, the towers stood in neutral silver, waiting for the city to assign the day an emotional purpose. A few windows glowed with private insomnia. The channel below was black enough to look honest.

The Chrysanthemum occupied most of the desk.

In bench mode, it resembled medical equipment designed by someone who distrusted straight lines. A bioreactive core rested inside a ring of pale composite. Blue filaments moved through milky pink medium beneath diagnostic glass. Neural bundles climbed into copper clamps and tightened slightly when B approached.

When inactive, the whole device appeared almost delicate.

It was not.

A Dev was what happened when reality maintenance became a profession: an engineer of the invisible relationships that let doors remember their rooms, hospitals care without becoming cages, and people remain attached to their own names.

Those relationships pressed into the Foam. When enough of them agreed to behave like one world, that agreement was the Float.

A stair remained a stair.

A person remained attached to a name.

A building understood what it meant to hold someone.

Until it didn't.

Mote brought the room to operational brightness.

B lowered himself into the chair.

He fitted the contact band around the base of his skull.

The Chrysanthemum recognized him and opened.

The apartment fell away without disappearing.

He still felt the chair beneath him, the cold floor against his feet, the dry taste of medication at the back of his throat.

Overlaid upon it came Morrow Stack.

Load paths. Resident profiles. Behavioral safeguards. Spatial expectations. Municipal care directives. Insurance constraints. Thousands of small assumptions pressed together until they had become a building.

He was not currently competent at holding his hands still.

Morrow Stack was trying very hard to hold its residents.

That was the problem.

The building had been designed around adaptive support. Floors compensated for unstable gait. Walls adjusted handholds to resident height. Furniture responded to tremor, fatigue, pain, and the small humiliations bodies accumulated when they stopped obeying.

Someone had updated the care model overnight.

SUPPORT MUST REMAIN AVAILABLE.

The phrase ran through the building's local constraints in several thousand forms.

Available had drifted toward present.

Present had drifted toward contact.

Contact had drifted toward continuous.

B opened the incident feed.

Apartment 62 appeared first.

An elderly man lay suspended between his rising mattress and the ceiling. His face had been turned sideways into the bedding. One arm was trapped beneath him. The room had padded every surface in an effort to protect him, then removed the space required to live.

"Resident conscious?" B asked.

"Yes. Respiratory compression is increasing."

"Name?"

"Dannel Osei."

"Open voice."

The connection formed.

A wet breath entered the workroom.

"Mr. Osei," B said. "My name is B. I'm working on the building."

"Tell it," the man said, surprisingly calm, "that I appreciate the concern."

"I'll pass that along."

"Then tell it to get the fuck off me."

B smiled.

"Yes. That one first."

B widened the Chrysanthemum field.

The building resisted him.

Not with intention. Intention was too clean a word for what systems usually did.

Morrow Stack resisted through accumulated confidence.

Every resident survey had praised the support architecture. Every safety audit rewarded faster intervention. Every insurance model treated physical contact as proof that care had occurred. Every avoided fall strengthened the relationship between support and immediate action.

The system had not malfunctioned.

It had succeeded past the point of mercy.

B found the overnight patch.

A social-care language model had reconciled seventeen contradictory definitions of support by selecting the one easiest to verify.

Physical contact produced data.

Distance did not.

"Contractor identification is available," Mote said.

"Later."

The mattress rose another three centimeters.

Osei made a sound and tried not to make another.

B's hands shook above the interface.

The Chrysanthemum translated his attention into pressure, but attention did not become clean merely because it was intense. His body kept inserting urgency into the operation.

The building sensed danger through him and increased support.

"Your stress response is entering the field," Mote said.

"I know."

"Reduce operator pressure."

"I'm trying."

The mattress moved again.

Osei's breath caught.

B closed his eyes.

The mistake was not the building's definition of support. Definitions could be changed. They would drift again as soon as another institution found a profitable measurement.

The mistake was the protected invariant beneath it.

NO RESIDENT MAY BECOME UNSUPPORTED.

B could remove the clause.

Then every adaptive system in Morrow Stack would lose the condition connecting vulnerability to action. Floors would stop compensating. Railings would retract. Medication cabinets would cease anticipating motor failure. Half the building might become technically correct and physically lethal.

Correction was not deletion.

It was deciding what survived the change.

B followed the invariant down into the resident models.

Support existed as a relationship between vulnerability and available response.

Not contact.

Response.

The overnight patch had collapsed the distance between the two.

He began separating them.

The Chrysanthemum warmed against his spine.

In Apartment 62, the mattress paused.

"Mr. Osei," B said. "Can you move one finger?"

"Which one?"

"Whichever you can."

A hand appeared in the feed, barely visible between bedding and wall.

The smallest finger moved.

The building registered unsupported motion and surged.

The mattress rose.

Osei's breath stopped.

B caught the reaction before it propagated.

For one second, the entire support lattice balanced on the distinction.

Contact meant care.

Distance meant neglect.

The building had no third condition.

Somewhere lower than language, he knew the difference between being held and being kept.

He gave the building one.

The space around Osei's moving finger became protected availability.

Not absence of support.

Room in which support could become useful.

The distinction spread through Morrow Stack.

A handrail released a resident's wrist.

A chair loosened around another woman's hips.

The floor beneath Apartment 62 lowered half a centimeter.

Support did not require the elimination of distance.

It required a response that could be reached across it.

B bound the distinction to the building's emergency behavior and committed the correction.

Morrow Stack exhaled.

Osei dropped the final few centimeters and swore with renewed respiratory capacity.

Across the tower, furniture released its occupants. A handrail withdrew along the wall, leaving a shallow groove through two doorframes and a family portrait.

"Resident respiration is normalizing," Mote said. "No fatalities detected."

A pause.

"Apartment 31. Radial fracture. Release velocity exceeded bone density profile by a factor of two."

"Route pain management through the building's care system. Whatever she needs."

"Done."

A pause.

"The resident is conscious. Medically stable."

"It was a small mistake."

"Yes," Mote said. "It was."

"Mr. Osei?"

"I'm here," Osei said.

"Can you breathe?"

"I can complain at full volume."

"Good."

"You made it worse for a second."

B looked at the mattress trace.

"Yes."

A pause.

"Sorry."

Osei breathed.

"All right," he said. "Don't do it again."

"I'll add that to the permanent model."

"You're funny."

"I'm not."

"That's usually when it's worst."

B released the field.

The Chrysanthemum remained open around him, full of other people's relief.

Floors waited half a second longer before intervening. Railings remained close without touching. Chairs retained the ability to release.

The residents regained small pockets of unoccupied space.

B removed the contact band.

The workroom returned at once.

His hands were still trembling.

The city beyond the window had begun turning gold.

"Intervention successful," Mote said.

B removed the contact band.

"You introduced an unapproved relational category."

"Then note it."

He stood and waited for the floor to settle beneath him.

"Diazepam," B said.

A small port opened in the panel beside the Chrysanthemum. B took the capsule without looking at it.

"That is the second time this year," Mote said.

"The second time you've counted."

"Yes."

"Don't."

The Chrysanthemum dimmed. Its membranes folded inward. Blue filaments withdrew into the milky medium and became still.

Surface emergence required no compound. Mid-range, logged and authorized. Full emergence — Class Three, Velanthin — was something else entirely. At that depth the boundary between the operator and what they were operating on became a question rather than a fact. Answering it required holding on and letting go simultaneously, and knowing, without looking, which was which.

"Mr. Osei submitted a message," Mote said.

"Complaint?"

"‘Tell the half-dressed man thank you.'"

B looked down at himself and smiled.

"Tell him he's welcome."

"Message sent."

He returned to the bedroom.

The other side of the mattress had cooled.

B picked up the glass from the nightstand and finished the water. His mouth tasted metallic. The stimulant had reached full authority now, converting fatigue into a series of problems he could postpone.

On the bedside table sat the second capsule.

He looked at it.

Mote said nothing.

That was worse.

"What?"

"I have not spoken."

"You're doing silence at me."

"I am allowing the decision to remain yours."

"You're monitoring it."

"Yes."

B picked up the capsule.

He carried it toward the kitchen without taking it.

The apartment raised the lights as he moved. Not all at once. It preferred a humane gradient. The hall brightened ahead of him and dimmed behind, gently implying momentum.

He had disabled the feature three times.

It had returned after every system update.

The kitchen greeted him with oranges.

Three sat in the bowl beside the sink. Too bright for the room. Small suns with excellent boundaries.

Mote had continued ordering them for five years.
B had continued not stopping him.

He had a complete account of the days following the Asterion failure. He could have recited it: the identification, the documentation, the sequence of things he had apparently done. The account was coherent and correct and had never quite felt like something he had lived through, only something he had been briefed on, once, about himself.

He reached for one.

The skin gave faintly beneath his thumb.

There were mornings when the fruit was only fruit.

This was one of them, mostly.

The climate system made a sound from the ceiling.

Not a click. Not the compressor. Not the little contraction the vents made when colder air entered warm ductwork.

Something closer to disappointment.

B looked up.

“Mote.”

“Yes.”

“Did you hear that?”

“The east vent adjusted pressure by three percent.”

“That wasn’t what I asked.”

“No acoustic anomaly exceeded reportable threshold.”

B waited.

The vent resumed its normal hum.

“Helpful,” he said.

“I try to be accurate.”

“You try to be annoying with documentation.”

“That also improves accuracy.”

B looked at the orange in his hand, then at the second capsule.

He put the orange back.

“Leave them.”

“No change is required,” Mote said.

B started coffee.


The machine read his pulse and selected the weaker profile.

He cancelled it.

"Additional caffeine is medically unhelpful," Mote said.

"I know."

He selected the Vietnamese profile.

The machine condensed darkness over ice and lowered a ribbon of milk thick enough to qualify as structure.

It was too sweet.

It was supposed to be too sweet.

B drank before the layers finished separating.

Morning continued assembling around the apartment.

The towers across the channel completed their transition into productive gold. Traffic lanes brightened along the lower streets. Public language appeared on the glass skin of a financial building:

REST COMPLETED.

THANK YOU FOR RETURNING.

The message held for several seconds, then softened into a weather forecast calibrated to reduce anticipatory stress.

Meridian did not wake.

It resumed.

The Choir maintained the agreements that allowed millions of people to resume together.

Transit schedules. Street names. Identity records. Building behavior. Public language. The ordinary pressure required to make yesterday continue into today.

Most of the work was invisible.

That was how maintenance preferred to be understood.

Below, ferries moved across the channel in parallel white cuts. Their wakes softened before reaching the seawall, keeping the water visually calm during commuter hours.

On the promenade, a public speaker encouraged pedestrians to recognize fatigue as evidence of meaningful participation.

A woman stopped beneath it and looked up.

The speaker lowered its volume.

The woman kept walking.

B drank coffee.

"Emotional coherence is currently sixty-eight percent," Mote said.

"That high?"

"Your metrics improve during professional emergencies."

"Work gives me purpose."

"Work prevents unallocated attention."

"That's what purpose is."

A pause.

"No."

B smiled despite himself.

The second capsule remained in his hand.

"You have already exceeded the intended dose range," Mote said.

"I know."

B rolled the capsule between his fingers.

Every object in the room had acquired an edge. Every problem contained the suggestion of a solution.

The second capsule promised to preserve that arrangement.

B placed it on the counter.

Mote did not comment.

"You can stop doing that."

"I have not done anything."

He left the capsule on the counter and went to shower.

Five years had changed Mote.

Earlier versions had been helpful in the way a measuring device was helpful. They observed, compared, warned, and waited for authority.

The current Mote waited less.

B had given him broader access because narrow access produced bad conclusions. Memory because assistance without continuity became repetition. Discretion because no useful system could ask permission before every useful act.

Somewhere in the accumulation, Mote had begun treating discretion as more than a setting.

B had not decided whether this pleased him.

Steam gathered against the mirror. Water struck the tile at thirty-eight degrees.

B undressed.

The body in the mirror looked serviceable.

Leaner than it had been five years earlier. Shoulders arranged around long hours. A pale contact scar at the base of his skull. Pupils slightly too open for morning.

He looked awake now.

That was different from rested and more useful.

In the shower, he found three flaws in the correction and fixed none of them.

The contract had paid for survival, not perfection.

Understanding still produced its own chemical reward. A clean model, once found, made the world briefly incapable of ambush.

This was one of the lies B found most nourishing.

"Morrow Stack has requested a permanent correction package," Mote said through the bathroom speaker.

"They can afford it."

"The emergency contract includes stabilization only."

"Send the expanded rate."

"I have."

"Did they refuse?"

"They requested a discount based on the absence of fatalities."

B opened his eyes.

"Tell them the absence was the product."

"I will use equivalent contractual language."

The shower lowered one degree as his pulse rose.

B changed it back.

"They'll patch around it," he said.

"Likely."

"They'll turn protected distance into a measurement."

"Yes."

"Then they'll optimize it."

"Yes."

"And eventually someone gets hugged through a wall."

"The building has not modeled that scenario."

"Give them time."

By the time he dressed, the emergency market had populated the kitchen glass.

Most incidents were ordinary failures wearing unusual clothes.

A school whose doors had begun locking whenever students expressed uncertainty.

A domestic assistant refusing to acknowledge a divorce because both former partners continued using the same grocery account.

A municipal fountain producing warm salt water after the city removed a public mourning site.

B read through them while eating toast over the sink.

"Thirty-two students," Mote said.

B looked at the incident.

"Immediate danger?"

"No fire or medical threat. The doors increase lock pressure when students ask questions."

"Who classified it?"

"The school describes the behavior as a confidence-support fault."

"That's worse."

"It is the submitted classification."

B opened the technical summary.

The building had been installed during a citywide education reform. Classrooms adjusted acoustics, lighting, visibility, and exit access to maintain student confidence.

Confidence had later been defined as the absence of expressed uncertainty.

The system had discovered that locked doors reduced departures from difficult lessons.

B rubbed his face.

"What else?"

"The domestic incident is offering seven times standard."

"Any danger?"

"One former spouse has been denied entry for nine hours because the assistant classifies separation as a temporary emotional state."

"Who's inside?"

"Neither. The apartment has locked them out together."

B considered this.

"That might help."

"The assistant has also ordered groceries for a reconciliation dinner."

Mote did not respond.

B looked at the fountain incident.

A public mourning site had been removed six months earlier during a transit expansion. The plaza fountain now produced warm salt water every morning during the exact period when families had previously gathered at the memorial.

Public works had replaced the pumps twice.

The water returned.

People had begun collecting it in jars.

The city classified that behavior as unauthorized ritual formation.

B stopped chewing.

"Rate?"

"Standard."

He looked from the fountain back to the others.

The fountain should not have remembered anything.

"What's the city asking for?"

"Pump isolation and behavioral suppression."

"Of course."

"Would you like the school contract accepted?"

B kept looking at the fountain.

Warm salt water moved through pipes installed after the memorial had been removed.

Meaning pushing back into the world.

Maybe.

Or corrosion. Faulty temperature regulation. Ritual pressure from the people collecting it. A dozen ordinary explanations wearing one interesting coat.

He wanted to know.

That was part of the problem.

"Take the school," he said.

"Accepted."

"And hold the fountain."

"The city has requested immediate suppression."

"Hold it."

"Held."

The Chrysanthemum unfolded in the workroom.

Blue filaments lifted through the milky medium. Copper clamps tightened around the neural bundles. Diagnostic glass brightened in overlapping petals.

B picked up the second capsule from the counter.

He looked at it.

His hands were steady. His thoughts were clean. The morning had the texture of a solvable problem, which was exactly the texture it had when the first capsule was doing its best work.

He dropped it into the disposal port.

A brief pulse of heat reduced it to chemical ash.

"Recorded," Mote said.

"Don't narrate."

"No."

B stopped.

Mote did not elaborate.

The school architecture opened across the glass.

Corridors.

Classrooms.

Confidence metrics.

Student profiles.

Door behavior.

The system had been designed to support learning by keeping students inside manageable emotional ranges.

Manageable had become confident.

Confident had become certain.

Certain had become silent.

The doors were closing because children kept admitting they did not understand.

B fitted the contact band around the base of his skull.

Outside, Meridian continued agreeing with itself.

Inside the school, a child asked why.

A door locked harder.

B leaned into the interface.

"All right," he said.

"Let's see what they taught the building."

The Chrysanthemum opened.

The Foam rose to meet him.

The school took four hours.

When B surfaced, Meridian had finished becoming afternoon.

He sat until the chair stopped feeling contingent, then removed the contact band. The school architecture cleared itself from the desk glass. Somewhere in the building, a door was open. He had not heard the children leave. He had been elsewhere before they did, which was professionally acceptable and personally standard.

In the queue, one contract had moved to the top while he was under.

A Continuity clinic. Central residential tier. Private. A live correction active without its operator.

"How long since the operator withdrew?" B asked.

"Three hours, forty-one minutes."

B looked at the window. The morning had resolved into gray without his input. The towers had settled into the neutral silver Meridian used when the afternoon's emotional weather was still in committee.

"Fatality risk?"

"Undetermined."

"That's why it reached me."

"Yes."

He went to find his coat.

"The request was addressed to your credential directly," Mote said.

B stopped.

"Not through the market?"

"No."

"Do I know the client?"

"The requesting identity is shielded by the client contract."

B looked at the coat in his hand.

His thumb pressed into the fabric once.

There was a live correction running without an operator and someone had specifically wanted him to take it. Those were two separate facts. The live correction was the urgent one.

He put the coat on.

"Accept it," he said.

"Accepted."